# ⛔⛔ THE LEXICON'S IDENTITY IS A HASH OF ITS RAW BYTES.
#
# `tlon.grammar.classes.load()` computes `blake2b(lexicon.yaml bytes)` and every
# Act-2 axis refuses to run unless the loaded hash equals the one it declares —
# "a run that silently used a different lexicon than it declared is a result
# about a different language."
#
# With `core.autocrlf=true` (the default on Windows) a fresh checkout rewrites
# 400 line endings and the file goes 8,414 -> 8,814 bytes. The hash changes from
# e2b8527010231a81fd31b6eeb9de3d8c to 56413e34caf21bb7800b558b6062daae, and
# 22 tests fail with `AxisError: declares lexicon ... but the loaded lexicon
# is ...`. So the language's identity depended on which OS cloned the repo.
#
# ⭐ Found 2026-09-04 while simulating a rented Linux box ON WINDOWS. The box
# was fine (Linux checkouts are LF); the SIMULATION was what broke, and it broke
# loudly enough to expose a real latent defect for anyone cloning on Windows.
# ⛔ `-text` disables conversion in both directions. The stored blob is already
# LF, so this changes no blob and no working copy — it only stops a future
# checkout from silently redefining the language.
tlon/grammar/lexicon.yaml -text

# ⛔⛔ THE EXPANDED LEXICON HAS THE SAME IDENTITY PROBLEM AND IT IS ALREADY LIVE.
# `pipeline_puzzle.sh` refuses to train unless the loaded hash equals
# 08c03b0a81330e4ba42883fa8b08c873, and the box clones this repo. Without this
# line a Windows checkout rewrites its 400+ line endings, the hash moves, and
# the preflight halts a run that is otherwise correct — or, worse on some other
# path, a run trains against a language whose identity nobody can reproduce.
# ⭐ Added the moment `git add` warned "LF will be replaced by CRLF" on it;
# that warning is the only signal this failure gives before the box.
tlon/grammar/lexicon_expanded.yaml -text

# ⭐ The carry gate's vocabulary carries root names (`flöx`, `hräx`) and is
# regenerated by `tools/act2_score_happening_words.py`. It has no hash gate of
# its own, so a checkout that rewrote its line endings would not fail loudly —
# it would simply produce a different diff on every regeneration and make the
# provenance harder to trust. Pinned for the same reason as the lexicon.
tlon/act2/happening_words.json -text

# Shell pipelines are executed on Linux boxes. Never let a checkout give them
# CRLF: `bash -n` tolerates it, and then a line like `for B in $BUILDS; do\r`
# fails at run time on a machine that bills by the hour.
*.sh text eol=lf

# ⛔⛔ THE PUZZLE CORPORA ARE BYTE-PINNED, SO A CHECKOUT MAY NOT REWRITE THEM.
# `pipeline_puzzle.sh` rebuilds the training rows on the box and refuses unless
# their sha matches the one whose root-carry and truncation figures are
# published. These three files are the inputs to that build. If a checkout gave
# any of them CRLF the rebuild would still parse them — Python's universal
# newlines hide it — but the repo would carry a different artifact than the one
# that was audited, and the next person to hash them would get a different
# answer for no stated reason. Same reason as the lexicon above.
runs/act2/corpus_conv_steered/*.jsonl -text
runs/act2/corpus_natural/*.jsonl -text
runs/act2/corpus_contrastive/*.jsonl -text
